# Independent chapter 19 generated-view review

Fixed source `e8c396ada1e3d48d981f7a43276889e691e1be1b`, NEPL3-doc-canonical-html-fragment. No blocking display/content issue found. No production, source, draft, registry or Git edits were made. This is independent verification of generated views against a fixed manuscript; this reviewer originally authored 19, so this is NOT a new independent semantic review of its own original writing. The unchanged manuscript and original Markdown retain the prior independent content review recorded in doc-authoring-delivery/19.

## Artifact and input identity

Nine HTML files (seven pages, CSS, manifest) from each output location are byte-identical. Eight Markdown files (seven pages, manifest) from each output location are byte-identical. Every manifest-listed artifact hash matches; every Markdown page equals its fixed Git blob. Every HTML manifest page input source hash equals its fixed Git `.nepld` input. The chapter19 Doc bytes equal the pre-cutover reviewed draft at 3abd255d exactly. Root-supplied generation was not independently rerun; these checks bind the inspected supplied outputs to their receipts and fixed source set.

## Local browsers

The existing project Playwright environment executed Chromium, Firefox and WebKit at 375/1280 widths and independent `/independent/deep/first/` and `/independent/deep/second/` prefixes with document JavaScript disabled. All twelve cases passed. Expectations were obtained from the fixed typed Doc structure, not the root test counts/results. All eighteen paragraph base strings, one heading, seventeen inline-code strings and five External href values match in order; all original Markdown code strings exactly equal the draft's seventeen code values. There are no Sections, lists or tables and therefore no semantic Section navigation cases to claim.

All 463 Ruby nodes are present, stylesheets load, IDs are unique, no document scripts or failed/nonlocal requests and no horizontal viewport overflow were observed. External URI fragments are preserved as part of the exact href comparison, including RFC section-5.2.3 and HTML table/a/ruby identifiers. External destinations were not visited to infer existence, availability, revision or normative currentness. The HTML does not receive a legacy article alias; this remains a Markdown compatibility feature.

## Actual fixed GitHub page

URL: https://github.com/neknaj/NEPL3/blob/e8c396ada1e3d48d981f7a43276889e691e1be1b/doc/spec/19-html-fragment.md . The saved actual browser HTML's rawLines reconstruct the full fixed generated Markdown bytes. The old `19-html-fragmentの構造契約` target is unique and actual navigation from an intentionally distant scroll position settles on a visible target with five stable observations. No semantic Section movement was fabricated for this chapter.

The actual GitHub DOM contains the five exact External hrefs and seventeen code strings. A separate full all-notes content assertion matches all eighteen paragraphs and the article title, including every Ruby reading, against the Doc tree. Exactly one empty compatibility-anchor paragraph is accounted for separately. This is viewing-profile preservation, not Doc roundtrip or accessibility equivalence.

The main check completed its twelve local cases and one GitHub navigation successfully, then its optional final screenshot reload timed out waiting for networkidle (30 seconds). Its script and raw log remain unchanged; the run is not labeled exit-zero. A separate bounded screenshot probe waited for DOMContentLoaded and the visible fixed article/title and successfully captured the viewport. `html-results.json`/`github-results.json`, the independent content log, and `github-shot.json` describe these different outcomes explicitly. No production change or cap increase was used to conceal the timeout.

## Visual inspection and pending scope

Inspected WebKit 375 title and link-region screenshots and 1280 link-region screenshot. Ruby readings remain aligned with their base, the long code/paragraph text stays within the viewport, and the RFC link label remains visibly distinct and wraps normally. The page is long with all readings retained; no text reduction was applied. This is not screen-reader, physical-device or human acceptance testing.

README restoration/bootstrap is not included yet: the archive README was unavailable during this initial pass. Root compiler tests, generation operation failures/resource profiles, deployment and main integration are separate evidence. Runtime implementation completion is not inferred from migration of this specification.

The manifest excludes redundant served trees, caches and full-page local screenshots. It includes fixed source, all input/output hashes, Markdown copies/receipts, scripts, raw logs, DOM/results, and selected viewport screenshots. UTF-8 logs with BOM from PowerShell are preserved unchanged as execution evidence; no source text was rewritten.

## Bootstrap follow-up

The now available archive README Python fence was frozen and executed verbatim in a fresh independent scratch repository layout containing only the payload index and the nine selected root-html-first payloads. It restored `dist/canonical19-snapshot`; all nine restored files equal the first supplied HTML bundle byte-for-byte and match the archive hashes. The exact snippet, README, index and restored hashes are retained.

Chromium, Firefox and WebKit then loaded the restored chapter with file URLs, JavaScript disabled and an explicit HTTP(S)-blocking route. All three passed with zero HTTP(S) attempts. All eighteen base paragraphs, one heading, seventeen code values and five External hrefs again match the typed Doc; no Section, list or table was fabricated. The local stylesheet is present, Ruby count463 and computed background/whitespace match the backend, with no viewport overflow. The URI destinations were not contacted. The initial pending-bootstrap record and manifest are preserved as initial-record.md and initial-manifest.json; initial manifest SHA256 is `256c818049c8d94389ad0f89cf526143d2d82ee05670f4fb8a7f6a6a122a20aa`.

The archive README also explicitly preserves the earlier AllocationLimit operation failure and finite pre-operation Markdown resource selection. Its root generation and fourteen-test claims remain root evidence, not a new independent runtime test claim. The optional screenshot-reload timeout from the main browser probe remains a recorded unsuccessful helper step, separate from the successful bounded screenshot follow-up and all required display/content/navigation checks.
